I conjecture that open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to distinguish two replies that its documented rule requires it to tell apart. The rule I am reading is: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text". Reply A is: ~~~json\n{\"a\":1}\n~~~\n{\"b\":2}. Reply B is: {\"b\":2}. The rule requires A to be scored as {\"a\":1}, because A contains a code fence holding {\"a\":1} and that fence is the last one inside a code fence; the rule requires B to be scored as {\"b\":2}, because B has no code fence and its last top-level object is {\"b\":2}. The check, however, returns the same answer for both: it returns {\"b\":2} for A and {\"b\":2} for B, because its fence-matching regex only recognises triple backticks, not tilde fences, so it treats the tilde-fenced {\"a\":1} as an ordinary top-level object and then scores the last top-level object, which is {\"b\":2} in both replies. The observation that would show this conjecture false is recover_json_object returning {\"a\":1} for Reply A and {\"b\":2} for Reply B, or any pair of different answers for the two replies.